WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.935
A capture comes in.

2
00:00:03.935 --> 00:00:07.099
The bus must hand it to everyone who asked for it.

3
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:09.715
Each handler subscribes under a type.

4
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:11.965
Ledger and receipts want captures.

5
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:14.281
Movements wants any money movement.

6
00:00:14.281 --> 00:00:15.869
Audit wants every event.

7
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.950
Refunds wants refunds only.

8
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.880
They all sit in a map, keyed by that type.

9
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:26.274
The bus from the generics workshop looks up one key: the event's own class.

10
00:00:26.274 --> 00:00:26.829
Predict.

11
00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:28.148
Which handlers run?

12
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:31.617
Only ledger and receipts.

13
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:33.838
Movements and audit never run.

14
00:00:33.838 --> 00:00:40.605
At run time, an event's class is concrete, like PaymentCaptured, never an interface like MoneyMovement.

15
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.002
So the bus works out which keys to look up.

16
00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:48.176
PaymentCaptured implements MoneyMovement, which extends PaymentEvent.

17
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:50.853
The walk starts at the event's own class.

18
00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:52.144
That's the first key.

19
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.287
Then it asks that class for its interfaces and its superclass.

20
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.264
Object is not an event, so it's skipped.

21
00:00:59.264 --> 00:01:00.452
MoneyMovement is.

22
00:01:00.452 --> 00:01:01.991
That's the second key.

23
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:04.892
Its supertype, PaymentEvent, is the third.

24
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:06.825
Three keys, nearest first.

25
00:01:06.825 --> 00:01:10.072
Siblings like PaymentRefunded are never touched.

26
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:14.711
A class's keys never change, so the bus caches them, once per class.

27
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.097
Now publish looks up just those three keys.

28
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:21.230
Captures first: ledger, then receipts.

29
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.400
Then movements.

30
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.257
Then audit.

31
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.425
Specific handlers run before general ones, and within one key, in subscription order.

32
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.301
Now audit itself.

33
00:01:31.301 --> 00:01:33.308
It's a Handler of PaymentEvent.

34
00:01:33.308 --> 00:01:39.201
Can it subscribe under PaymentCaptured, where the bus expects a Handler of PaymentCaptured?

35
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.731
A handler only takes events in.

36
00:01:42.731 --> 00:01:47.277
Audit takes any event, so the captures this subscription brings are fine.

37
00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:52.145
So Handler is declared in, and one general handler fits any subscription.

38
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:55.604
Delete the in, and the same line fails to compile.

39
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:56.896
Java writes ?

40
00:01:56.896 --> 00:01:59.257
super E at each use instead.

41
00:01:59.257 --> 00:02:03.557
kotlinc puts that wildcard in for Java callers too.

42
00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:07.711
Here is one step of the walk, in Java and Kotlin.

43
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:11.055
The rule.

44
00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:15.498
Look up the event's class, then each event supertype, nearest first.

45
00:02:15.498 --> 00:02:18.700
A handler only takes events in, so declare it in.

46
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:19.900
Check yourself.

47
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.036
A PaymentDeclined is published.

48
00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:24.241
Which keys does the bus look up?

49
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:27.198
Two.

50
00:02:27.198 --> 00:02:30.100
PaymentDeclined, then PaymentEvent.

51
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.011
Audit runs.

52
00:02:31.011 --> 00:02:34.659
Movements doesn't: a decline moves no money.
